WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.768
Trainee pilots practise in a flight simulator. They can face an engine failure safely, again and again, without anyone getting hurt. A computer simulation lets us try out situations that would be dangerous, costly or impossible in real life.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:26.161
A model is a set of rules and data that represents a real system. A simulation runs the model, to see what happens over time.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:38.825
Here is a tiny model in Python. The rule is: rabbits double every year. Running it is the simulation. Three years pass in a moment: two hundred, four hundred, then eight hundred.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:51.923
Simulations have big benefits. They are safe. You can change variables to ask what if. They can run faster or slower than real time. And they help to predict outcomes and test ideas.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:08.585
But a simulation is only as good as its data and rules. Real rabbits do not double for ever, so our model is wrong after a few years. Big simulations also need a powerful processor, because they handle lots of data and many calculations.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:22.969
First, decide what is asked: a benefit, a limit, or a hardware need. State one clear point. Then expand it with a linked effect in the scenario. Never repeat a benefit the question has already given.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:39.951
Here is a real question from Paper 1 in June 2024. Peter is creating a simulation of rabbits and foxes. One benefit is that it is simpler than watching real animals. Explain one other benefit. It is worth two marks.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:47.483
Simpler is already used, so we choose a different benefit. The simulation can run much faster than real life.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:56.135
Now link it to Peter's scenario. Years of rabbit and fox numbers can be seen in minutes, so he can spot long-term trends quickly.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:12.999
So the answer is: the simulation can run much faster than real life, so Peter can see many generations of rabbits and foxes in a short time, and spot long-term trends. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.433
A common mistake is giving two separate facts instead of one point and its linked effect.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.720
Another mistake is saying a simulation is always accurate. Its results depend on its data and rules.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:39.306
Now you try. A town uses a traffic simulation to plan new traffic lights. Explain one reason why the town should not fully trust its results. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:55.042
The point is a limit: the simulation is only as good as its data and rules. Then the link: if the traffic data or the assumptions about drivers are wrong, the predicted queues will be wrong too, so the lights could be planned badly.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:04.752
Remember the method: decide what is asked, state one point, then link it to the scenario with so. Now try the practice questions for this type.
